12 2026-09-24] NVIDIA expects FY2028 revenue growth of about 70%; the article says supply constraints could limit the pace of meeting demand.\n    Quote: NVIDIA also expects fiscal 2028 revenue growth of about 70%, although supply constraints could limit how quickly it meets demand.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e46, e196; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e135 [third_party_view; Zacks.com; original source AMD ; from gnews:7fb0b2b7d22b8bb07625b212 2026-09-24] AMD's Helios platform is in production, with initial shipments expected late in 2026 Q3 and a larger ramp-up expected in Q4 2026 and 2027.\n    Quote: Helios is in production, with initial shipments expected late in the third quarter and a larger ramp-up in the fourth quarter and 2027.\n- e136 [fact; Zacks.com; original source Anthropic ; from gnews:7fb0b2b7d22b8bb07625b212 2026-09-24] Anthropic plans to deploy up to 2 GW of AMD MI450-series GPUs on Helios, with the first 1 GW planned to begin in the first half of 2027.\n    Quote: Anthropic plans to deploy up to 2 gigawatts of MI450-series GPUs using the AMD Helios rack-scale platform, with the first gigawatt beginning in the first half of 2027.\n- e137 [fact; Zacks.com; original source Microsoft ; from gnews:7fb0b2b7d22b8bb07625b212 2026-09-24] Microsoft plans to deploy AMD's Helios platform at scale on Azure.\n    Quote: Microsoft plans to deploy Helios at scale on Azure.\n- e138 [party_statement; Zacks.com; original source AMD ; from gnews:7fb0b2b7d22b8bb07625b212 2026-09-24] AMD expects its Data Center segment revenue to more than double year over year in 2027.\n    Quote: Advanced Micro Devices expects Data Center segment revenues to more than double year over year in 2027.\n- e157 [third_party_view; Trefis, the article’s author; original source Trefis, the article’s author 2026-09-24; from gnews:06218d9421731e5e59c1dbef 2026-09-24] Trefis reported that consensus estimates had NVIDIA earnings and revenue growing at a similar pace from FY2027 to FY2028, implying roughly steady profit margins.\n    Quote: Consensus has earnings and revenue growing at a similar pace between fiscal 2027 and fiscal 2028, so analysts assume profit margins hold roughly steady.\n- e159 [party_statement; semafor.com; original source Jensen Huang ; from gnews:73ad0caddfdd9ae934f00920 2026-09-24] Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang said Nvidia’s chip sales would double next year; the item does not specify the sales measure or year.\n    Quote: Huang, who said that Nvidia would double its chip sales next year\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e99; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e161 [fact; Cloud Wars, reporting the announcement; original source AWS and NVIDIA 2026-09-24; from gnews:7c4283c55cf02ba9cfd9a835 2026-09-24] AWS and NVIDIA announced a plan to deploy 2 million additional NVIDIA GPUs across AWS infrastructure between 2027 and 2028.\n    Quote: Now, the two powerhouses have announced a staggering expansion of this existing partnership which will, among other things — but this is certainly the standout headline — include the deployment of 2 million additional NVIDIA GPUs across AWS infrastructure between 2027 and 2028.\n- e162 [fact; Cloud Wars, reporting the announcement; original source Amazon Robotics and NVIDIA 2026-09-24; from gnews:7c4283c55cf02ba9cfd9a835 2026-09-24] Amazon Robotics plans to integrate NVIDIA’s full-stack physical AI platform into its robotics projects.\n    Quote: On the physical AI side, Amazon Robotics will work with NVIDIA to integrate its full-stack physical AI platform, helping ramp up the development of Amazon’s robotics projects.\n- e163 [fact; Cloud Wars, reporting the announcement; original source AWS and NVIDIA 2026-09-24; from gnews:7c4283c55cf02ba9cfd9a835 2026-09-24] AWS and NVIDIA plan to build AI factories for the U.S. government, enabling delivery of 100,000 GPUs on AWS secure infrastructure for federal and national-security workloads.\n    Quote: AWS and NVIDIA will build AI factories for the U.S. government, enabling the delivery of “100,000 GPUs on AWS’s secure infrastructure for federal and national-security workloads.”\n- e181 [party_statement; trefis.com; original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:a6d9db55f2f71f072c5ba33c 2026-09-25] NVIDIA management expects supply to remain a bottleneck at least through the end of fiscal 2028.\n    Quote: Management expects supply to remain a bottleneck at least through the end of fiscal 2028.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e154, e197, e319; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e186 [party_statement; trefis.com; original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:a6d9db55f2f71f072c5ba33c 2026-09-25] NVIDIA management expects AI labs backed by NVIDIA’s balance sheet to account for roughly one-quarter of NVIDIA’s business in fiscal 2028.\n    Quote: Management expects AI labs backed by NVIDIA’s balance sheet to make up roughly a quarter of NVIDIA’s business in fiscal 2028.\n- e187 [third_party_view; trefis.com; original source trefis.com 2026-09-25; from gnews:a6d9db55f2f71f072c5ba33c 2026-09-25] The author calculated NVIDIA revenue growth of 83% over the past year, compared with 16.7% for Texas Instruments.\n    Quote: NVIDIA’s revenue grew 83% over the past year, against 16.7% for TI.\n- e189 [party_statement; MarketBeat; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:91b917994e36036e304616e8 2026-09-25] MarketBeat reports that NVIDIA forecasts doubling its chip volume next year (2027).\n    Quote: NVIDIA's (NASDAQ: NVDA) forecast to double its chip volume next year strengthens an already bullish growth outlook for the company and its stock.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e99, e345; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e196 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source Colette Kress, NVIDIA CFO ; from gnews:4c4c114117f5c7acb7be91f7 2026-09-25] NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress said the company expects fiscal 2028 revenue to grow roughly 70%.\n    Quote: And on the company's late-August earnings call, CFO Colette Kress said Nvidia expects revenue to rise roughly 70% in fiscal 2028, the year ending in January 2028.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e132; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e197 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source Colette Kress, NVIDIA CFO ; from gnews:4c4c114117f5c7acb7be91f7 2026-09-25] NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress characterized the fiscal 2028 revenue outlook as supply-constrained, with manufacturing capacity limiting revenue rather than customer demand.\n    Quote: She called it \"a supply-constrained outlook.\" Put another way, the limit is manufacturing capacity, not customer demand.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e181; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e199 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source fool.com (analyst) 2026-09-25; from gnews:4c4c114117f5c7acb7be91f7 2026-09-25] The author calculated that applying roughly 70% growth to the $412 billion fiscal 2027 consensus estimate implies around $700 billion of NVIDIA fiscal 2028 revenue.\n    Quote: Grow that by roughly 70%, and fiscal 2028 lands somewhere around $700 billion.\n- e201 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source fool.com (analyst) 2026-09-25; from gnews:4c4c114117f5c7acb7be91f7 2026-09-25] The author expects NVIDIA's earnings to come in more or less as forecast.\n    Quote: I think the earnings will show up more or less as forecast.\n- e246 [party_statement; fool.com; original source Broadcom ; from gnews:030fdadd39d106b9a582bbe5 2026-09-26] Broadcom expects AI semiconductor revenue of $115 billion in FY2027 and $230 billion in FY2028.\n    Quote: For fiscal year 2027, Broadcom expects to generate $115 billion in AI semiconductor revenue. In FY 2028, that