e Nvidia chips moving forward.\n- e287 [party_statement; The Motley Fool author, reporting Musk's comments; original source Elon Musk ; from gnews:33aa3209db96726fbc8fdc92 2026-09-27] Musk said SpaceX expected to reach around 2 GW of computing power by the end of 2026 and was targeting closer to 10 GW than 5 GW by the end of 2027.\n    Quote: At the time, Musk said SpaceX would end the year with around 2 gigawatts of computing power, while it is looking to end 2027 with closer to 10 gigawatts than 5 gigawatts.\n- e288 [third_party_view; The Motley Fool author; original source The Motley Fool author 2026-09-27; from gnews:33aa3209db96726fbc8fdc92 2026-09-27] The author estimated that 1 GW of computing power costs about $50 billion to $60 billion, including about $35 billion of Nvidia chips and other components.\n    Quote: One gigawatt of compute power costs about $50 billion to $60 billion, with chips and other Nvidia components making up about $35 billion of that total cost.\n- e319 [party_statement; trefis.com; original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:d4f3bddf04a4d029a097541c 2026-09-27] NVIDIA management expected supply to remain a bottleneck at least through the end of fiscal 2028.\n    Quote: Management expects supply to stay a bottleneck at least through the end of fiscal 2028.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e181; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e325 [third_party_view; How-To Geek author Monica J. White; original source How-To Geek author Monica J. White 2026-09-27; from gnews:382aee425dc6b77586e1c456 2026-09-27] How-To Geek reported that the retail prices of NVIDIA’s RTX 5060 Ti 16GB and RTX 5070 Ti had risen to around $800 and $1,200, respectively, from launch MSRPs of $429 and $749; the article gives no specific date for the price observations.\n    Quote: Just to give you a couple of examples, the RTX 5060 Ti 16GB launched with a $429 MSRP, and it now costs around $800 , while the RTX 5070 Ti launched at $749 but now costs around $1,200 .\n- e345 [party_statement; cnbc.com; original source Jensen Huang ; from gnews:02b796f20d201c8380e0ae4d 2026-09-28] NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang said the company would double the number of chips it sells in calendar 2027.\n    Quote: Huang earlier this month said Nvidia would double the number of chips it sells in 2027.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e189; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n\n[Themes in force, 5]\n- v14 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will China's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology permit Alibaba and ByteDance to buy Nvidia RTX PRO 5500 cards by January 31, 2027?: now rung 1 Yes; judged 2026-09-28; reasons: The reference is rung 2 (no permission by the deadline): no market consensus or confirmed permission is supplied, so the reference is the no-permission status. The forecast is rung 1 because the report that MIIT intends to approve purchases, together with its request for Alibaba's and ByteDance's pl\n- v15 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will China's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology approve domestic firms' purchases of Nvidia RTX Pro 5500 GPUs by January 31, 2027?: now rung 1 Yes; judged 2026-09-27; reasons: The reference is rung 2 because no market consensus or formal approval timetable is supplied, so the no-approval-by-deadline outcome is the baseline rather than a reported expectation. The evidence shifts the forecast to rung 1: the reported request for purchase plans and stated intent to approve ma\n- v6 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will the U.S. administration authorize Nvidia Blackwell chip exports to China by January 31, 2027?: now rung 1 No; judged 2026-09-28; reasons: The available reference is the existing U.S. restriction on Nvidia's most advanced chips, which places the expectation at rung 1; no Blackwell-specific consensus or guidance is supplied. The evidence implies that authorization of Blackwell by January 31, 2027 remains less likely than non-authorizati\n- v7 (ai-capex, due 2027-04-30) How much will the five largest AI hyperscalers spend on data centers in calendar 2026?: now rung 3 $775 billion to below $825 billion; judged 2026-09-26; reasons: The reference is nearly $800 billion for calendar 2026, which sits in rung 3 ($775 billion to below $825 billion). The evidence still implies a total near $800 billion, so it does not cross either boundary. The energy comments and financing concerns identify possible constraints or risks but give no\n- v8 (ai-capex, due 2028-12-31) How much will tech companies spend on AI in calendar 2027?: now rung 3 Middle range; judged 2026-09-28; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is about $1.3 trillion for 2027, which sits in rung 3 ($1.2 trillion to below $1.4 trillion). The direct estimate for tech-company AI spending remains about $1.3 trillion; the hyperscaler capex figures support the scale but are not added to that broader estimate. S&P Global Ratings' pr\n\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 2; reasons: The market reference is Zacks' FY2028 estimate of $671.95 billion, which sits in rung 3. The stronger forecast input is NVIDIA's roughly 70% FY2028 growth outlook: applying it to the $412 billion FY2027 consensus base implies about $700 billion, in rung 2. The announced AWS GPU deployments add evidence of demand, but do not state revenue or provide a price-to-revenue conversion; the company also expects supply constraints through FY2028. The implied figure therefore remains above rung 2's $692.1085 billion floor but below rung 1's $739.145 billion floor.\n\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views must be empty: this thesis depends on no other thesis; theme_pushes only the theme ids listed above; a theme id goes only in theme_pushes, never in cites or cites_views.\n```\n\n### The stored answer of question P (2026-09-28)\n\n- Answer: 2\n- Reference: NVIDIA's FY2028 revenue market estimate is $671.95 billion (e5), placing the reference in rung 3: at least $651.7915 billion and below $692.1085 billion.\n- What it expects: About $700 billion: apply management's roughly 70% FY2028 growth outlook to the $412 billion FY2027 consensus base ($412 billion × 1.70), as calculated in e199. This is above rung 2's $692.1085 billion floor but below rung 1's $739.145 billion floor.\n- Reasons: The market reference is NVIDIA's FY2028 revenue estimate of $671.95 billion, which is rung 3. The stronger forecast input is management's roughly 70% FY2028 growth outlook: applying it to the $412 billion FY2027 consensus base gives about $700 billion, in rung 2. That estimate is close to rung 2's $692.1085 billion floor: using Zacks' lower $406.05 billion FY2027 estimate instead would imply about $690 billion, just below the boundary. The additional customer-demand announcements support the outlook but do not give a reliable FY2028 revenue conversion; the SpaceX target is a capacity ambition, not confirmed NVIDIA revenue, and management expects supply constraints through FY2028. The central forecast therefore remains rung 2, with limited margin over rung 3.\n\n## Question Q\n\n**How much will Nvidia's total revenue grow in FY2028?**\n\nOpened at article 22 of the week; due 2028-02-29; 43 statements linked, 39 of them printed in this request. Possible answers:\n\n- 1 Strongly bullish: FY2028 total revenue growth is at least 80%.\n- 2 Bullish: FY2028 total revenue growth is at least 70% and below 80%.\n- 3 Neutral: FY2028 total revenue growth is at least 45% and below 70%.\n- 4 Bearish: FY2028 total revenue growth is at least 25% and below 45%.\n- 5 Strongly bearish: FY2028 total revenue growth is below 25%.\n\n### The request of question Q's latest judgement (2026-09-28), as the judge received it\n\n```\nThesis v5 (NVDA, asset, focus \"FY2028 total revenue growth\", FY2028, due 2028-02-29): How much will Nvidia's total revenue grow in FY2028?\nLadder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: FY2028 total revenue growth is at least 80%. / rung 2 Bullish: FY2028 total revenue growth is at least 70% and below 80%. / rung 3 Neutral: FY2028 total revenue growth is at least 45% and below 70%. / rung 4 Bearish: FY2028 total revenue growth is at least 25% and below 45%. / rung 5 Strongly bearish: FY2028 total revenue growth is below 25%.\n\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 39 items]\n[Not shown: 4 more pieces in force, read in earlier judgements; the previous conclusion carries them]\n- e13 [party_statement; barchart.com (analyst); original source Colette Kress ; from gnews:355e074116b277efe4dcaaab 2026-09-22] Nvidia CFO Colette Kress guided for 70% revenue growth in FY2028, versus 45% previously modeled by Wall Street, according to the author.\n    Quote: CFO Colette Kress guided for 70% revenue growth in fiscal 2028 — a figure that far exceeded the 45% Wall Street had previously modeled.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e44; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e44 [party_statement; barchart.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:aa0d7cffe1e3c710b6347ad4 2026-09-23] Nvidia guided to about 70% revenue growth for FY2028.\n    Quote: issued bullish guidance for about 70% revenue growth in fiscal 2028\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e13, e46, e56, e62, e70, e152, e360, e371; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e46 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst), quoting NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress on the fiscal 2027 second-quarter earnings call; original source NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress ; from gnews:46b3435210a9cc3557b44d78 2026-09-23] NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress said customer forecasts point to NVIDIA's sales growth doubling in FY2028, while NVIDIA expects approximately 70% growth because it is supply-constrained.\n    Quote: Customer forecasts point to our growth doubling next year. However, as I mentioned earlier, we expect to grow approximately 70% as we are supply-constrained.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e44, e63, e77, e132; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e59 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source Wall Street analysts ; from gnews:b27439abc0c0e249a9a8bbc9 2026-09-23] The author says Wall Street expects Nvidia revenue growth of 91% in FY2027 and 66% in FY2028.\n    Quote: Wall Street expects the company to grow revenue by 91% this year and 66% next year.\n- e62 [party_statement; Colette Kress, quoted by fool.com (Daniel Sparks); original source Colette Kress, Nvidia CFO ; from gnews:ef6c9a018d21f7762df0efde 2026-09-23] Nvidia CFO Colette Kress expected Nvidia revenue to grow approximately 70% in FY2028.\n    Quote: We expect to grow revenue by approximately 70% in fiscal 2028,\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e44; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e63 [party_statement; Colette Kress, quoted by fool.com (Daniel Sparks); original source Colette Kress, Nvidia CFO ; from gnews:ef6c9a018d21f7762df0efde 2026-09-23] Nvidia CFO Colette Kress characterized the company's FY2028 revenue outlook as supply-constrained.\n    Quote: This is a supply-constrained outlook.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e46, e154, e321; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e64 [party_statement; Jensen Huang, quoted by fool.com (Daniel Sparks); original source Jensen Huang, Nvidia CEO ; from gnews:ef6c9a018d21f7762df0efde 2026-09-23] Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang said customer demand without the supply constraint would be a lot higher.\n    Quote: would be a lot higher.\n- e90 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source Keithen Drury 2026-09-23; from gnews:915fe84b4c7f7b4decd1665d 2026-09-23] The author said Broadcom and its AI hyperscaler clients are designing workload-specific AI computing units that perform AI computing at lower cost and more effectively than Nvidia's GPUs, threatening Nvidia's market share.\n    Quote: There are also workload-specific AI computing units being designed by Broadcom ( AVGO +2.83% ) and its AI hyperscaler clients that perform AI computing at lower cost and more effectively than Nvidia's or AMD's GPUs, threatening Nvidia's market share.\n- e94 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source Nvidia ; from gnews:915fe84b4c7f7b4decd1665d 2026-09-23] Nvidia projected that global annual data-center capital expenditures would reach $3 trillion to $4 trillion by 2030.\n    Quote: Ultimately, Nvidia sees global annual data center capital expenditures increasing to $3 trillion to $4 trillion by 2030.\n- e132 [party_statement; Zacks.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:7fb0b2b7d22b8bb07625b212 2026-09-24] NVIDIA expects FY2028 revenue growth of about 70%; the article says supply constraints could limit the pace of meeting demand.\n    Quote: NVIDIA also expects fiscal 2028 revenue growth of about 70%, although supply constraints could limit how quickly it meets demand.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e46, e196; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e152 [party_statement; Trefis, relaying NVIDIA management’s guidance; original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:06218d9421731e5e59c1dbef 2026-09-24] NVIDIA management guided to about 70% total revenue growth in FY2028, its first full-year-ahead revenue growth guidance.\n    Quote: For fiscal 2028, management has guided about 70% revenue growth, the first time it has guided a full year ahead.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e44; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e153 [party_statement; Trefis, relaying NVIDIA’s CEO; original source NVIDIA CEO ; from gnews:06218d9421731e5e59c1dbef 2026-09-24] NVIDIA’s CEO said demand supports revenue growth well above 70% for FY2028, but supply limits the guidance to about 70%.\n    Quote: The CEO says demand is much greater than 70%, and that supply is what holds the guide there.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e56; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e154 [party_statement; Trefis, relaying NVIDIA’s CFO; original source NVIDIA CFO ; from gnews:06218d9421731e5e59c1dbef 2026-09-24] NVIDIA’s CFO expected supply to remain a bottleneck through at least the end of FY2028 and described memory pricing as extreme.\n    Quote: The CFO expects supply to stay a bottleneck at least through the end of fiscal 2028 and calls memory pricing extreme.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e63, e181; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e157 [third_party_view; Trefis, the article’s author; original source Trefis, the article’s author 2026-09-24; from gnews:06218d9421731e5e59c1dbef 2026-09-24] Trefis reported that consensus estimates had NVIDIA earnings and revenue growing at a similar pace from FY2027 to FY2028, implying roughly steady profit margins.\n    Quote: Consensus has earnings and revenue growing at a similar pace between fiscal 2027 and fiscal 2028, so analysts assume profit margins hold roughly steady.\n- e159 [party_statement; semafor.com; original source Jensen Huang ; from gnews:73ad0caddfdd9ae934f00920 2026-09-24] Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang said Nvidia’s chip sales would double next year; the item does not specify the sales measure or year.\n    Quote: Huang, who said that Nvidia would double its chip sales next year\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e99; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e161 [fact; Cloud Wars, reporting the announcement; original source AWS and NVIDIA 2026-09-24; from gnews:7c4283c55cf02ba9cfd9a835 2026-09-24] AWS and NVIDIA announced a plan to deploy 2 million additional NVIDIA GPUs across AWS infrastructure between 2027 and 2028.\n    Quo