abs backed by NVIDIA’s balance sheet to make up roughly a quarter of NVIDIA’s business in fiscal 2028.\n- e187 [third_party_view; trefis.com; original source trefis.com 2026-09-25; from gnews:a6d9db55f2f71f072c5ba33c 2026-09-25] The author calculated NVIDIA revenue growth of 83% over the past year, compared with 16.7% for Texas Instruments.\n    Quote: NVIDIA’s revenue grew 83% over the past year, against 16.7% for TI.\n- e196 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source Colette Kress, NVIDIA CFO ; from gnews:4c4c114117f5c7acb7be91f7 2026-09-25] NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress said the company expects fiscal 2028 revenue to grow roughly 70%.\n    Quote: And on the company's late-August earnings call, CFO Colette Kress said Nvidia expects revenue to rise roughly 70% in fiscal 2028, the year ending in January 2028.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e132; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e197 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source Colette Kress, NVIDIA CFO ; from gnews:4c4c114117f5c7acb7be91f7 2026-09-25] NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress characterized the fiscal 2028 revenue outlook as supply-constrained, with manufacturing capacity limiting revenue rather than customer demand.\n    Quote: She called it \"a supply-constrained outlook.\" Put another way, the limit is manufacturing capacity, not customer demand.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e181; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e199 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source fool.com (analyst) 2026-09-25; from gnews:4c4c114117f5c7acb7be91f7 2026-09-25] The author calculated that applying roughly 70% growth to the $412 billion fiscal 2027 consensus estimate implies around $700 billion of NVIDIA fiscal 2028 revenue.\n    Quote: Grow that by roughly 70%, and fiscal 2028 lands somewhere around $700 billion.\n- e201 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source fool.com (analyst) 20